{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1529,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists used to think that the universe\nhad existed forever, unchangingly, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5999,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's what their observations of the night\nsky suggested. Needless to say, this view"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10059,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clashed with the \"origin\" or \"creation\" stories\nof most major religions, which hold that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14780,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe had a beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16090,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it's not surprising that it was a Catholic\npriest, Georges Lemaître, who was one of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19790,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first major proponents of a new scientific\nviewpoint - that the universe DID have a beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24700,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lemaître, of course, was also an excellent\nmathematician and scientist and based this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28650,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conviction not (just) on his religious beliefs\nbut upon new experimental evidence from Edwin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33000,
    "dDurationMs": 4089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hubble that showed the universe was expanding.\nThis evidence, combined with the mathematics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37089,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of general relativity allowed Lemaître to\n\"rewind\" cosmic history and calculate that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41499,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the farther back in time you go, the smaller\nthe universe had to be. The natural conclusion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46249,
    "dDurationMs": 4121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that everything we can currently see in\nthe universe was at one point in time more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50370,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or less at one point in space. Lemaître called\nthis idea the \"primeval atom\", but of course"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55670,
    "dDurationMs": 2729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today we know it as \"the big bang theory\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58399,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except \"big bang\" is a horrible name - it\nwould be much more accurate to call it \"the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62999,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everywhere stretch\". Because one of the most\ncommon misconceptions about the big bang is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66770,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it implies that the entire universe was\ncompressed into a single point from which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70390,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it then somehow expanded into the surrounding...\nnothingness? It is true that the observable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75939,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, that is, the part of the whole universe\nwe can see from earth, WAS indeed shrunk down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80189,
    "dDurationMs": 4881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a very very small bit of space, but that\nbit of space was NOT a single point, nor was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85070,
    "dDurationMs": 2899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rest of the Universe also in that same\nbit of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87969,
    "dDurationMs": 5061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The explanation for this is the magical power\nof infinity. The whole universe is really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93030,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big - current data show it's at least 20 times\nbigger than the observable universe, but that's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97619,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a lower bound - it might be infinite.\nAnd if you have an infinite amount of space,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102200,
    "dDurationMs": 4279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can scale space down, shrink everything\nto minuscule proportions, and still have an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106479,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite amount of space. Kind of like how\nyou can zoom out as much as you want from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110270,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a number line, but it'll still be an infinite\nnumber line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113560,
    "dDurationMs": 4989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially, space doesn't need anywhere to\nexpand \"into\" because it can expand into itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118549,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and still have plenty of room. In fact, this\nis possible even if space turns out not to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123049,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be infinite in size, though the reasons are\ncomplicated and have to do with the infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126829,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "differentiability of the metric of spacetime..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129289,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But anyway, the event unfortunately known\nas the big bang was basically a time, long"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133590,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago, when space was much more squeezed together,\nand the observable universe, that is, everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138050,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we see from earth, was crammed into a\nvery very small piece of space. Because the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142870,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ENTIRE early universe was dense and hot everywhere,\nspacetime was curved everywhere and this curvature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148250,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manifested itself as a rapid expansion of\nspace throughout the universe. And although"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152930,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people call this \"the big bang\", it wasn't\njust big, it was everywhere. And it wasn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158390,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really an explosion - it was space stretching\nout. It's really quite unfortunate that \"the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163220,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everywhere Stretch\" isn't nearly as catchy\nas \"the Big Bang\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166680,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which brings us to the \"big bang singularity\",\nwhich is an even horribler name because every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171200,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single word is misleading. I mean, \"singularity\"\nseems to imply something that happened at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176140,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single point. Which isn't at all what it's\nreferring to - it SHOULD be called \"the part"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180640,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Everywhere Stretch where we don't know\nwhat we're talking about.\" Basically, our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184370,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current physical models for the universe are\nunable to properly explain and predict what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188519,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was happening at the very very beginning when\nthe universe was super SUPER scaled down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193209,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But rather than call it the \"time when we\ndon't have a clue what was happening, ANYWHERE\","
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196989,
    "dDurationMs": 2591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for some reason we call it a \"singularity\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199580,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This ignorance, however, does conveniently\nanswer the question What happened BEFORE the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203260,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big bang? Because it tells us the question\nisn't well defined - back when space was so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207819,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incredibly compressed and everything was ridiculously\nhot and dense, our mathematical models of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212430,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe break down SO MUCH that \"time\"\ndoesn't even make sense. It's kind of like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217120,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how at the north pole, the concept of \"north\"\nbreaks down - I mean, what's north of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221870,
    "dDurationMs": 4729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "north pole? The only thing you can say is\nthat everywhere on earth is south of the north"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226599,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pole, or similarly everywhen in the universe\nis after... the beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231459,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But once time began, whenever that was, space\nexpanded incredibly quickly all throughout"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236040,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe - for a little while. Then expansion\nslowed, the universe cooled, stuff happened,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240909,
    "dDurationMs": 2711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and after a few billion years, here we are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243620,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One thing we still DON'T know is why this\nEverywhere Stretching happened - that is,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247870,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why did the universe start off in such a funny,\ncompressed state, and why did it follow the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252239,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seemingly arbitrary laws of physics that have\ngoverned its expansion and development ever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256329,
    "dDurationMs": 690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257019,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For Georges Lemaître, this might be where\nGod finally comes into the picture to explain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261310,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the things science can't. Except that experimental\nevidence doesn't actually rule out the possibility"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266710,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there may indeed be a time \"before\" the\nbeginning, a previous age of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270940,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that ended when space collapsed in on itself,\ngetting quite compressed and dense and hot,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275550,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but not enough to mangle up our ideas of what\ntime is. It would have then bounced back out,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280530,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretching in a fashion similar to what we\ncall the big bang, but without the \"we don't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284250,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know what we're talking about\" singularity\npart. So, physics may actually be nudging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288520,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us back to the view that the Universe is eternal\nand didn't begin after all. In which case"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293690,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Professor Lemaître might have to rethink\nhis interpretation of the words \"in the beginning.\""
    } ]
  } ]
}
